3. The facts relevant for the purpose of disposal of the Rule are that the petitioner is the local shipping agent of the vessel MT Jamac of Liberia, which carried Crude Degummed Soyabean Oil (CDSO) in bulk for Bangladesh and that on arrival of the cargo at the Port of Chittagong a cargo manifest was submitted showing 17,150 metric tons of CDSO as the cargo carried for Bangladesh and that the quantity was surveyed on 24-8-92 at Chittagong Port under Ullage Survey method by an approved surveyor on board the vessel and the quantity was found at 17,049.57 metric tons and accordingly the difference between the declared manifested quantity of 17,150 metric tons and the Ullage Surveyed quantity of 17,049.57 metric tons was found to be 100.43 metric tons which was less than 1% of the difference allowed as Ocean allowance and that thereafter, the CDSO was discharged from the vessel to the shore tanks and accordingly, dry tanks certificate was issued by the Master of the vessel as by the surveyor certifying that the entire CDSO carried for Bangladesh Port has been discharged from the vessel and that thereafter another survey was conducted by dip reading of the shore tanks and a shortage of 236.709 metric tons was found on the basis of difference between the manifest declared quantity and the shore tank survey. report and accordingly, the Assistant Commissioner of Customs asked the petitioner to show cause about such shortage, to which the petitioner duly replied explaining the position denying their liabilities but the Adjudication Officer (respondent No. 7) without considering the Ullage Survey report and the dry tanks certificate imposed penalty of Taka 42,50,989.50 for the alleged shortage of 236.709 metric tons of CDSO relying on the shore tank survey report vide order dated 5-3-98 and being aggrieved there against the petitioner preferred Appeal under section 193 of the Chairman Act before the respondent No. 2, who upheld the order of the respondent No. 3 in part vide order No. 87/97 dated 29-4-97 reducing the penalty amount from Taka 42,50,989.50 to Taka 25,25,593. Being aggrieved the petitioner there- after preferred appeal there against before the respondent No. 1 Tribunal and the respondent No. 1 passed the impugned order dated 5-3-98 upholding the order passed by the respondent No. 2. The Appellate Tribunal erred in misreading the provisions of item No. 24(ii) of the table of section 156(1) of the Customs Act, 1969 to the effect that any shortage, as to the quantity, which has to be found in the conveyance, if cannot be accounted for to the satisfaction of the Customs authority, the carrier will be liable under the said provisions and that the Appellate Tribunal failed to consider the fact that, declared manifested quantity was 17,150 metric tons of CDSO imported for Bangladesh and as per Ullage Survey the quantity was found to be 17,049.57 metric tons thereby, there was a shortage of 100.43 metric tons, which quantity was less than 1% comparing to the total quantity of 17,150 metric tons and 1% of shortage is within permissible limit as Ocean allowance and that the quantity of CDSO carried for Bangladesh Port as per Ullage Survey at the port of loading was 17063.52 metric tons and therefore, the actual shortage is (17,063.52-17,049.57) metric tons i.e. 13.95 metric tons, both measured under Ullage Survey method, and which is less than the permissible limit.&nbsp; 

 5. Submitted that in the instant case the purpose of shortage, 1% of the imported quantity carried by the vessel is allowed as Ocean allowance must be taken into account, and that the admitted position is that as per declared manifest the quantity imported for Bangladesh was 17,150 metric tons of CDSO and as per Ullage Survey the quantity found at the Port of Chittagong was 17,049.570 metric tons and thus there was difference of only 100.43 metric tons, which was much less than 1%, the permissible limit and this shortage do not attract the aforesaid provisions of the Customs Act. The learned Advocate submitted that the claimed shortage quantity of 236.709 metric tons has been calculated on the basis of the survey made on dip tank reading at shore, which is not permissible under any provisions of law. It has been submitted that the measuring to determine any actual shortage has to be made in the basis of measurement taken on the basis of same type of calculation or method. In the instant case the survey has been made as per Ullage method both at the port of loading and at the port of unloading, hence the said survey reports have to the taken into consideration for determination of the shortage, if any, of the CDSO carried by the carrier to the port of Chittagong. As per Ullage Survey, conducted at the port of loading, the quantity of CDSO carried for Bangladesh was 17063.520 metric tons and at the port of unloading at Chittagong the quantity was found to be 17,049.570 metric tons and thus, there was shortage of only 13.95 metric tons, which is much below the permissible 1% Ocean allowance. The Ullage survey is conducted on board the ship and such survey is universally accepted for which in the aforementioned provisions of the Customs Act, it has been categorically mentioned that the quantity of shortage, if any, has to be found in the conveyance but the Customs Authority as well as the Appellate Tribunal failed to consider this aspect of the law and accordingly, arrived at a wrong decision. The learned Advocate in support of his contention referred to a decision of the High Court Division of this Court in the case of James Finlay PLC vs Member (appeal and revision) and Ex-Officio Additional Secretary and others, passed in writ petition No. 70 of 1986 vide judgment and order dated on 13-6-90, wherein also a shortage claim was made by the customs authority on the basis of shore tank measurement and the same was declared to be without lawful authority in making the Rule absolute, wherein their Lordships have accepted the submission of the learned Advocate that the Ullage survey having not been considered by the Customs Authority, the shortage as clamed and the penalty as imposed were bad in law, inasmuch as for the purpose of determining shortage, the shore tank survey was not contemplated under item No. 24(ii) of the table under section 156(1) of the Customs Act, 1969 and 156 held that:&nbsp; 

 &quot;In the light of the provision of clause 25(ii) of the table under section 156(1) of the Customs Act, 1969 and in the light of definition of the &#39;conveyance&#39; as quoted above it can be said that the shortage, if any, has to be found on board the vessel. Therefore, the Ullage Survey Report on Board the vessel, &quot;Botany Trojan&quot; shall prevail against the shore tank survey report in case of any difference in respect of shortage of cargo between the Ullage Survey Report on board the vessel and the Shore Tank Survey Report and that in case the shortage, if any, found in the Ullage Survey on board the vessel is covered by the 1% admissible Ocean allowance then the earlier will not be responsible for any shortage of crude oil found in the Shore Tank Survey Report which is not covered by 1% admissible Ocean allowance and in such case no penalty can be imposed on the carrier or his agent. The respondents have not applied their mind to this aspect of the case.&quot;&nbsp; 

 8. The admitted facts are that the vessel MT Jamac carried cargo for two countries, namely, Bangladesh and Singapore. The cargo for Bangladesh Port was Crude Degummed Soyabean Oil (CDSO) and as per declared manifest the cargo quantity carried for Bangladesh was 17,150 metric tons, whereas as per Ullage Survey conducted at the port of loading the quantity measured was 17,063.52 metric tons and that as per Ullage Survey conducted at the port of delivery, that is, at Chittagong Port the quantity was measured at 17,049.570 metric tons and that as per dip reading shore tank measurement the quantity delivered was measured at 16,913.291 metric tons. The learned Advocate submitted that Ullage Survey is a method by which the measurement of the liquid cargo carried by a vessel is taken on board the vessel, which is universally accepted. Thus, as per Ullage Survey the quantity of cargo carried for Bangladesh was 17,063.526 metric tons and the cargo carried to the port of Chittagong was found to be 17,049.57 metric tons and thus there was actually a shortage of only 13.95 metric tons, which is negligibly less than the permissible limit of 1% allowed as Ocean allowance. It further appears that the difference, between the declared manifest quantity of 17,150 metric tons and the Ullage Survey Report quantity of 17,049.570 metric tons, found at Chittagong comes to 100.43 metric tons, which is also within the permissible limit of 1% allowance as Ocean allowance. The difference between the declared manifest quantity and the dip reading shore tank measurement quantity shows a shortage of 236.709 metric tons. The learned Advocate referring to the provisions of item No. 24(ii) of the table under section 156(1) submitted that such shortage on the basis of shore tank reading is not contemplated by section 156 of the Customs Act and that section 156 of the Customs Act contemplates that measurement must be taken on the conveyance, that is, on board the vessel. In order to appreciate the said submission of the learned Advocate the relevant provision of law i.e. item No. 24(ii) of the table under section 156(1), is reproduced below:&nbsp; 

 9. On plain reading of the aforesaid provision of item No. 24(ii) we do not find any ambiguity in the law in accepting the submission of the learned Advocate for the petitioner inasmuch as it has been clearly mentioned that &quot;If the quantity found in the conveyance is short&quot;, which means the measurement has to be taken on board the vessel which carried the cargo. It further appears from the materials on record that for carrying the cargo (CDSO), the vessel is allowed 1% in the difference of measurement as to the quantity carried, as Ocean allowance. Now considering the difference between quantity carried (17,063.52 mt) and delivered (17,049.57 mt) both as per the Ullage Survey Report i.e. survey conducted, at the port of loading and at the port of delivery respectively, which was calculated at (17,063.52 mt-17,049.57 mt) 13.95 of metric tons and which is much less than the permissible limit of 1% and the difference between the declared manifest quantity (17,150 mt) and the Ullage Survey Report quantity (17049.57) at the port of delivery was 100.40 metric tons, which is also less than 1% compared to the quantity of CDSO carried by the vessel for Chittagong. Thus we do not find any such shortage for which the vessel can be held responsible for an offence as contemplated under item 24(ii) of the table under section 156(1) of the Customs Act. The measurement of the shore tank has no relevance in the instant case. We get support from the decision to which the learned Advocate referred to being the judgment and order dated 13-6-90 passed in Writ Petition No. 70/1986, wherein their Lordships in making the Rule absolute have held that in item 24(ii) of the table under section 156(1) of the Customs Act, 1969 the words &quot;quantity found in the conveyance&quot; means the quantity found as per measuring taken on board the ship as per Ullage Survey method. Therefore, the Ullage Survey Report on board the vessel prevails against the Shore Tank Survey Report. The question of shortage as contemplated by item No. 24(ii) of the table under section 156(1) of the Customs Act, the 1% admissible Ocean allowance on the quantity carried by the vessel must be taken into consideration, and therefore, the vessel/carrier cannot be held responsible for any shortage below 1% and such shortage cannot be said to be an offence committed under item No. 24(ii) of the table under section 156(1) of the Customs Act, 1969.&nbsp; 

 10. On perusal of the impugned order it appears that the Tribunal has taken notice of the report of the Ullage Survey conducted both at the port of loading and at the port of delivery but the Tribunal due to non-application of mind as to the provision of item No. 24(ii) of the table under section 156(1) of the Customs Act failed to consider that the shortage, if any, has to be calculated on the basis of the measurements taken on board the vessel taken as per Ullage Survey conducted both at the port of loading and at the pod of delivery and such a shortage cannot be calculated on the basis of survey conducted at the shore tanks as has been held by their Lordships in the judgment and order dated 13-6-90 passed in Writ Petition No. 70 of 1986 as mentioned earlier. Thus we find merit in the Rule.&nbsp;
